module runtime.abi_stdio

/// THE ARCHITECTURE SEAM: every write of a standard stream in the ported
/// runtime goes through these two functions. Nothing else in `pickle/` may call
/// `win_get_std_handle` or `win_write_file` on a standard handle.
///
/// WHY IT IS ITS OWN MODULE. This file is the only place where "which OS am I
/// running on" is decided for console output. `runtime.abi_fs` owns the file
/// system, `runtime.abi_raw` owns the heap, and `runtime.abi_os` owns process
/// termination - each already isolates one Win32 surface. Console output was the
/// one remaining surface spread across four files (`abi_console`,
/// `abi_exception`, `cli/main`, `cli/history`), so a non-Windows port had to
/// edit all four, two of which sit in the self-hosted CLI. Gathering them here
/// reduces that edit to one file.
///
/// IT DECLARES NO `#[symbol]` SLOTS. A `#[symbol]` fn is activated by an import
/// (measured, see `abi_exception`'s header), so a module that declares one costs
/// the importing program an entry in its unresolved set. These are ordinary
/// functions called by name, so importing `runtime.abi_stdio` activates nothing:
/// `linkall_all` imports it and its unresolved set is unchanged. That is what
/// makes it safe for `cli/main` to import - the self-host gap is measured in
/// whole symbols and this module adds none.
///
/// WHAT A LINUX PORT CHANGES. Only the two bodies below, to `write(1, ...)` and
/// `write(2, ...)`, plus the handle constants (a POSIX build has no handle at
/// all, so the constants go unused and the bodies take a fixed fd). The callers
/// keep passing a payload pointer and a byte count, which is already the shape
/// every libc and Win32 write wants. Nothing above this module knows that
/// `GetStdHandle` exists.
///
/// WHY THE POINTER IS A RAW `int` AND NOT A `*int`. The callers compute the
/// payload address with `+ STR_BYTES_OFF` themselves, because the string layout
/// is a *layout* question (`runtime.layout`), not an OS question. Keeping the
/// offset arithmetic outside means a change to string headers moves only the
/// layout-dependent lines and leaves this file's contract untouched.

/// `STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE`, spelled the way the SDK does: `(DWORD)-11`.
const STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE: int = -11

/// `STD_ERROR_HANDLE`, spelled the way the SDK does: `(DWORD)-12`.
const STD_ERROR_HANDLE: int = -12

/// Write `n` bytes starting at `ptr` to stdout.
///
/// One `WriteFile`, no buffering and no flush, mirroring the reference route:
/// `console.rs::write_to_con` -> `write_stdout` -> freestanding
/// `nostd::console_write`. Because nothing is buffered, `pickle_flush` is
/// redundant on this path - which is why `abi_console` can port it as a no-op.
fn writeStdout(ptr: int, n: int) -> int {
    unsafe {
        var h = win_get_std_handle(STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE)
        let _ = win_write_file(h, ptr, n, 0, 0)
        return 0
    }
}

/// Write `n` bytes starting at `ptr` to stderr.
///
/// Same route as `writeStdout` over `STD_ERROR_HANDLE`, mirroring
/// `nostd::console_error_write`. Callers write stderr through here rather than
/// through the console module so that an exception report never depends on the
/// module that `DO NOT IMPORT` rules constrain.
fn writeStderr(ptr: int, n: int) -> int {
    unsafe {
        var h = win_get_std_handle(STD_ERROR_HANDLE)
        let _ = win_write_file(h, ptr, n, 0, 0)
        return 0
    }
}
